<script lang="buff" props="Props">
    // typed_props.buffhtml — T134 component-model example 2.
    //
    // Exercises the `props="Props"` interface declaration. The script
    // body declares `struct Props { name: String, count: i32 }` and
    // codegen:
    //   1. Hoists the struct to module scope.
    //   2. Switches the signature to `fn Greeting(props: Props)`.
    //   3. Splices `let Props { name, count, .. } = props;` at fn
    //      entry so the rsx!{} body can reference `name` + `count`.
    //
    // The parent (rendered below the component definition) invokes
    // `<Greeting name="World" count: 42 />` — the prop type
    // pre-checker (item 3 of T134) accepts this call because:
    //   - `name` is required + provided + the literal `"World"` is a
    //     String literal (matches the declared type).
    //   - `count` is required + provided + the literal `42` is an
    //     integer literal (matches the declared `i32`).

    // Declared interface — hoisted to module scope by codegen.
    #[derive(Clone, PartialEq)]
    struct Props {
        name: String,
        count: i32,
    }

    // The body statements below are spliced into the component fn
    // AFTER the auto-generated `let Props { name, count, .. } = props;`
    // destructure. They reference `name` + `count` directly.
    let greeting = format!("Hello, {}! You have {} messages.", name, count);
</script>

<div class="greeting">
    <h2>{greeting}</h2>
</div>
